  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Scattered to numerous showers and a few embedded\nthunderstorms are expected ahead of a cold front through the \nearly evening. \n\nCurrent surface analysis (06/18z) suggests that the cold front \nis entering the interior of the forecast area. The front is \nexpected to steadily move through the forecast area this \nafternoon and offshore this evening. There is good model \nagreement that the scattered to possibly numerous showers and \neven a few thunderstorms will accompany the front, and current \nradar already depicts some isolated to scattered ongoing \nactivity. The most coverage is expected further north and will \nprimarily impact southeast SC and more specifically Colleton \nCounty and the Charleston Tri- County region. Regarding \nthunderstorm potential, temperatures warming into the low 80s \ncoincident with dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s will be \nsufficient to yield MLCAPE values into the 1,000-1,500 J/kg \nrange. So, we will likely see a few thunderstorms with the best \nchances in the area of greatest coverage previously mentioned. \nWind fields are not particularly impressive and the best forcing\nand severe environment is expected to remain off to the north \nacross the Pee Dee, Grand Strand, and eastern NC. Rainfall \npotential remains limited, with moderate potential for amounts \ninto the 0.25-0.50\" range with the best coverage and presence of\nthunderstorms. Elsewhere, localized amounts of 0.10-0.25\" will \nbe more likely. Any remaining showers and thunderstorms will \nshift offshore by the mid evening hours and the rest of the \novernight will be dry. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures \nexpected this weekend and into next week. \n\nBehind the departing cold front, expansive high pressure is \nexpected to build in across the forecast area for the weekend \nand continue into next week. The forecast is dry and no rainfall\nis expected through the middle of the week. Saturday will bring\ncooler temperatures, though highs should still get to around \nclimo normals for early November. Sunday could actually be \nslightly below normal with a much cooler start than we have seen\nrecently. Sunday morning lows are forecast to be in the low to \nmid 40s away from the coast, which should continue again Monday \nand Tuesday mornings. Afternoon highs will rebound nicely each \nday with widespread mid to upper 70s expected Monday through \nWednesday.",
